VideoHelper: a closed video window resets the place

IVLCVout's contract: a display of 0x0 means the video window closed. libVLC 4's Android display
(modules/video_output/android/display.c, Close) sends the last place along with it, and
VideoHelper kept that place, so the video SurfaceView stayed pinned to the old size. Where libVLC
places the picture itself (ASurfaceControl, Android 10 and later), nothing sizes the surface
again: once a stream changed its format (an HLS variant switch closes the video output), turning
the phone left the picture at the portrait size. Measured on the emulator, 10.11.2026.

Upstream (VLC and libvlcjni master, 10.11.2026) still has both halves. Drop this patch once
libvlcjni's VideoHelper honours a 0x0 display itself.

--- a/libvlc/src/main/java/org/videolan/libvlc/VideoHelper.java
+++ b/libvlc/src/main/java/org/videolan/libvlc/VideoHelper.java
@@ -271,6 +271,12 @@
     @Override
     public void onNewVideoLayout(IVLCVout vlcVout, int displayWidth, int displayHeight,
                                  int placeWidth, int placeHeight, int placeX, int placeY) {
+        /* A display of 0x0 means the video window closed (IVLCVout's contract), but libVLC 4
+         * sends the last place along with it. Kept, that place pinned the surface to the old
+         * size, and where libVLC places the picture itself (ASurfaceControl) nothing sized it
+         * again: after a stream's format changed, turning the phone left the picture small. */
+        if (displayWidth == 0 || displayHeight == 0)
+            placeWidth = placeHeight = placeX = placeY = 0;
         mPlaceWidth = placeWidth;
         mPlaceHeight = placeHeight;
         mPlaceX = placeX;
